FIELD MOUSE GOTCHAS
====================

Baseline: dilapidated-shed/fieldmouse master at
3736fe4088787f3fb16af64e2dabe75cd7ef15a4 (2026-10-12).
This is a plain-text compatibility and operational warning list.
Sources inspected: Fieldmouse.idric, Main.idric, Tests.idric, README.md,
ISSUES.md, tests/helpers/, and the repository CI definition.

Classification:
  ABSENT      No support in the current interpreter.
  DIFFERENT   Implemented but not standard JavaScript behavior.
  LIMITED     A real feature, with a narrower contract.
  GOAL        A desired future direction, NOT a current feature.
This list is intentionally expandable; it is not a complete ECMAScript audit.

START HERE
----------
DIFFERENT: This is NOT currently a drop-in JavaScript engine, nor V8,
  Node, a browser JS runtime, or the old C MuJS implementation. The active
  interpreter is written in Idriç and uses its own source language.
GOAL: The original intention included accepting unchanged JavaScript as well
  as experimenting with a changed syntax. A separate conventional-JS mode
  has NOT been delivered. Merely naming a file .js does not activate one.
DIFFERENT: Standard JavaScript examples copied from the web commonly fail
  to parse, or run with different semantics. Do not claim ES5/ES6/Node
  compatibility merely because examples of arrays and functions work.

OPERATORS AND LEXING
--------------------
DIFFERENT: "name ← value" and "value → name" assign. JavaScript "name = value"
  is not assignment here; "=" is an equality test.
DIFFERENT: "=" is a coercive-equality relation, "≠" its inequality, and "≟"
  strict equality. The coercive relation is only a partial implementation
  of JavaScript's conversion rules.
ABSENT: The JS equality spellings ==, !=, ===, and !== are not accepted as
  expression operators, although the lexer can recognize those tokens.
DIFFERENT: False is spelled Ø in Field Mouse source. "false" is NOT the
  Boolean false literal. "true" is supported.
LIMITED: Source arithmetic accepts +, -, *, / and numeric comparisons.
  Remainder "%" parses but evaluation fails with an explicit error.
DIFFERENT: The Idriç source implementation uses ÷ internally, but Field
  Mouse source division is /. Do not copy Idriç arithmetic syntax into .fm.
ABSENT: ++, --, +=, -=, *=, /=, ??, ?., ? :, bitwise operators, and
  JavaScript arrow functions (=>) are not supported.
LIMITED: Numeric literals are a basic decimal form with optional fraction.
  Do not assume JS hex, binary, exponent, leading-dot, or bigint literals.
  JSON parsing has a separate, more complete JSON number grammar.
LIMITED: Source strings support a small escape set (e.g. \n, \r, \t,
  quotes, and backslash). Do not assume JavaScript's \uXXXX or \xHH
  string literal behavior; JSON strings use a separate Unicode-aware parser.
ABSENT: Template literals, regexp literals, destructuring, spread/rest
  syntax, and computed keys inside object literals.
LIMITED: A trailing comma in an array literal is rejected. Do not assume
  JavaScript-style trailing commas or automatic semicolon insertion.

DECLARATIONS, CONTROL, AND ERRORS
---------------------------------
DIFFERENT: var, let, and const are provisional aliases. const does not
  enforce immutability, and let does not implement block scoping or a TDZ.
DIFFERENT: Blocks execute in the surrounding binding scope. JavaScript
  declaration hoisting is not implemented; functions become available when
  their declarations execute.
DIFFERENT: Reading an unknown variable fails. Assignment to an unknown
  variable presently creates a binding instead of raising ReferenceError.
ABSENT: for, for...of, for...in, do/while, switch, break, continue,
  throw, try/catch/finally, and JavaScript exception objects/handling.
LIMITED: if/else, while, return, and regular function declarations /
  function expressions work, but return outside a function fails.
LIMITED: Function calls require exactly the declared parameter count;
  JS allows missing or extra arguments. Duplicate parameter names fail.
LIMITED: Function call depth is checked and stops at 256.
ABSENT: Classes, constructors/new, prototype inheritance, generators,
  async functions, eval, Function constructor, and automatic method "this".
DIFFERENT: Calling an object property holding a function does not bind
  an implicit "this" receiver.
DIFFERENT: Errors abort the interpreter. They are not catchable by scripts.
  Source parsing finishes before effects start, but a later runtime error
  does not undo earlier file/process effects.

PROMISES, ASYNCHRONY, AND PLATFORM APIs
---------------------------------------
ABSENT: Promise constructor, Promise.resolve/reject, .then/.catch/.finally,
  microtasks, event loop, async/await, and task scheduling. Promises are
  not present with odd behavior: they are NOT implemented.
ABSENT: setTimeout, setInterval, queueMicrotask, browser events, DOM,
  fetch, XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, and browser storage APIs.
ABSENT: Node require(), import/export, ES modules, CommonJS modules, npm,
  node:fs, node:path, node:child_process, streams, process/Buffer APIs.
LIMITED: Instead use the project's own synchronous host functions such as
  readText, writeText, arguments, environment, and subprocess; these are
  not drop-in Node API substitutes.
ABSENT: Typed arrays, ArrayBuffer, binary Buffer, Symbol, BigInt, Map,
  Set, Date, regular-expression objects, and a general JavaScript
  built-in object/library surface (Math, Array methods, String methods,
  Object methods, and so forth).
LIMITED: console.log exists, but this is not a full browser/Node console.

OBJECTS, ARRAYS, STRINGS, AND COERCION
--------------------------------------
LIMITED: Arrays and objects have identities and support nested reads/writes,
  but no prototype chain, getters/setters, Proxy, or prototype methods.
ABSENT: Familiar array methods such as push, pop, map, filter, reduce,
  sort, splice, join, forEach, etc.
DIFFERENT: Array length is readable but assignment to length fails. An
  out-of-range nonnegative integer write grows the array and creates holes.
DIFFERENT: Array indices must be canonical nonnegative integer keys; JS
  allows ordinary extra array object properties such as array.foo.
LIMITED: Object property access supports text or nonnegative-integer keys.
  JavaScript's general ToPropertyKey conversion is not implemented.
DIFFERENT: Reading a missing object property/array slot yields undefined;
  reading a completely unknown global name fails.
LIMITED: Text exposes .length only; do not assume text[0] or JS String
  methods. Unicode length is not promised to follow UTF-16 JS code units.
DIFFERENT: Non-primitive values render in console output as [array],
  [object], [function], or [native ...], not Node inspection strings.
LIMITED: Numbers use Double, and formatting is runtime-specific: e.g.
  42 often displays as 42.0. Strict/loose comparison is not a complete
  ECMAScript conversion model. Do not assume JavaScript NaN/Infinity globals.
LIMITED: && and || short-circuit and return operand values, but broader
  JavaScript operator behavior is not implied.

JSON
----
DIFFERENT: Call parseJson(text) and stringifyJson(value), not JSON.parse
  or JSON.stringify. Those JavaScript global methods are not defined.
LIMITED: parseJson accepts proper JSON including JSON's true/false tokens
  and Unicode escapes; this does not make false a Field Mouse source literal.
DIFFERENT: stringifyJson refuses undefined, function values, nonfinite
  numbers, and cyclic references; JavaScript JSON.stringify has some
  omission/substitution behaviors that differ.
DIFFERENT: Missing array slots serialize as null. Repeated acyclic object
  references can serialize; cycles cannot.
DIFFERENT: Numeric text, object property order, and formatting are not
  promised byte-for-byte identical to JavaScript JSON.stringify.

HOST EFFECTS AND SECURITY
-------------------------
LIMITED: Text I/O is synchronous. There is no file removal, rename,
  recursive mkdir, filesystem stats, or general Node fs module here.
LIMITED: createDirectory creates a single directory. listDirectory
  supplies sorted names; lexical path helpers do not consult disk state.
LIMITED: NUL bytes in host file text/path arguments are rejected.
LIMITED: subprocess(program, arrayOfTextArgs) returns {status, stdout}.
  stderr is inherited, not returned. No stdin or streaming/process handle.
DIFFERENT: The underlying subprocess implementation currently uses an
  escaped shell-backed adapter. Arguments are individually quoted, but
  this is NOT direct exec-vector lowering or a security sandbox.
IMPORTANT: Filesystem/process effects can alter the host. Do not execute
  untrusted Field Mouse scripts. Typed host requests are not capability
  restrictions on allowed paths or programs.
DIFFERENT: console.log appends to an internal output list. The CLI prints
  collected logs after successful execution (including deliberate exit).
  If a runtime error happens, collected log output is discarded.
DIFFERENT: The CLI currently prints error diagnostics with putStrLn
  (stdout), not the stderr behavior JavaScript CLI users may expect.

RUNTIME, DELIVERY, AND EVIDENCE
-------------------------------
LIMITED: The active host runtime is compiled through Idriç to a Chez
  payload plus launcher/support files. It does not require Node or the
  Idriç compiler at execution, but it DOES need the documented Chez
  runtime and generated files. It is not a standalone ELF.
LIMITED: The supported CI platform is Linux x86-64. Android packaging
  and actual MIRO A1/C67 acceptance have not been established here;
  there is no Android/DEX requirement for the current Linux runtime.
LIMITED: Interpreter-owned aggregate/function identities remain allocated
  for the script lifetime. No claim of general-purpose garbage collection,
  long-running server suitability, or V8-like speed.
LIMITED: Node differential checks cover specific representative helper
  tasks, NOT general JavaScript language compliance.
IMPORTANT: Cat Food integration remains stale (old fieldmouse branch and
  incompatible syntax in its smoke test); do not interpret standalone
  Field Mouse CI success as a green end-to-end Cat Food install.
IMPORTANT: The active Idriç rewrite, inherited MuJS history, and existing
  upstream notice files require a separate attribution/license review
  before describing a polished public distribution. Do not discard
  historical licenses.

WHEN YOU FIND ANOTHER SURPRISE
------------------------------
Add a reproduction to Tests.idric or tests/helpers/, characterize its
observable result, then add an ABSENT, DIFFERENT, or LIMITED entry here.
Only call something GOAL when it has not shipped. Keep this file useful to
a reader deciding whether an ordinary JS script can run unchanged.
